{
  "concept": "An angle of depression is measured down from the horizontal at the observer, and two such sightings from one airplane to two points on a straight road create two right triangles that share the same altitude leg — which is what lets you solve for the plane's height and slant distances.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Small jetliner icon at upper left, nose pointed left, serving as the common vertex of both sight lines",
    "Teal dashed horizontal reference line extending right from the airplane at flight altitude",
    "Solid black horizontal line across the lower portion representing the straight highway",
    "Point marker and label 'A' on the highway, toward the middle",
    "Point marker and label 'B' on the highway, at the far right",
    "Gray dashed sight line from airplane down to A (steeper)",
    "Gray dashed sight line from airplane down to B (shallower)",
    "Angle arc at the airplane between the teal horizontal and the sight line to B, labeled '25°'",
    "Angle arc at the airplane between the teal horizontal and the sight line to A, labeled '49°'",
    "Vertical dashed altitude segment from the airplane down to the highway (appears in later phases), labeled h",
    "Right-angle square where the altitude segment meets the highway",
    "Horizontal ground distance brackets: airplane's ground point to A, and A to B labeled 'AB'",
    "Slant distance labels along the sight lines (distance to A, distance to B), shown in the unknowns phase",
    "Equation callout box showing tan(49°) = h / x_A and tan(25°) = h / (x_A + AB)",
    "Legend / color key: teal = horizontal reference, gray dashed = lines of sight, black = highway, orange = unknown quantities"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "altitude",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Airplane altitude h (miles)",
      "range": [0.5, 5, 0.1],
      "default": 2.5,
      "teaches": "Raising the plane steepens BOTH sight lines at once, so both depression angles grow together — the two angles are not independent, they are locked to a single shared height."
    },
    {
      "id": "abDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Distance from A to B along the highway (miles)",
      "range": [0.5, 8, 0.1],
      "default": 3.2,
      "teaches": "Sliding B farther down the road flattens only the far sight line and shrinks only the 25° angle, showing that the gap AB is exactly the quantity that ties the two right triangles together."
    },
    {
      "id": "phase",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Solution stage",
      "states": ["horizontal reference", "two right triangles", "label unknowns", "write equations"],
      "default": "horizontal reference",
      "teaches": "The standard setup for an angle-of-depression problem: measure angles from the horizontal, drop an altitude to create right triangles, name the unknown horizontal legs, then write one tangent equation per triangle."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The horizontal reference",
      "narration": "Angles of depression are always measured downward from the horizontal through the observer, not from the ground. The teal dashed line is that horizontal; the sight line to A drops 49° below it and the sight line to B only 25°.",
      "state": { "phase": "horizontal reference", "altitude": 2.5, "abDistance": 3.2 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Drop the altitude: two right triangles",
      "narration": "Dropping a perpendicular from the plane to the highway splits the picture into two right triangles that share the same vertical leg h. The nearer, steeper sighting gives the small triangle; the farther, shallower one gives the large triangle.",
      "state": { "phase": "two right triangles", "altitude": 2.5, "abDistance": 3.2 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Name the unknowns",
      "narration": "The horizontal leg to A is x, so the horizontal leg to B is x + AB. Only the spacing between the mileposts is known — everything orange is unknown.",
      "state": { "phase": "label unknowns", "altitude": 2.5, "abDistance": 3.2 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Raise the plane",
      "narration": "Push the altitude up and watch both angles open wider simultaneously. One height controls both triangles, which is precisely why two sightings are enough to pin it down.",
      "state": { "phase": "label unknowns", "altitude": 4.2, "abDistance": 3.2 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Push B down the road",
      "narration": "Now hold the height fixed and slide B away. Only the far angle changes, collapsing toward the horizontal — the 25° reading is really a statement about how far B is, given the same h.",
      "state": { "phase": "label unknowns", "altitude": 2.5, "abDistance": 6.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two tangents, one system",
      "narration": "tan(49°) = h/x and tan(25°) = h/(x + AB). Two equations, two unknowns: eliminate x to get h = AB / (cot 25° − cot 49°), then recover the slant distance to A.",
      "state": { "phase": "write equations", "altitude": 2.5, "abDistance": 3.2 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Both sight lines leave the same airplane, so the two right triangles share one vertical leg — the shared altitude is the bridge that converts two depression angles and one known ground gap into every missing distance.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape scene. Upper left: a small stylized blue-gray jetliner with nose pointing left; its nose/tail region is the common vertex for all rays. From that vertex a teal dashed horizontal line runs all the way to the right edge. A solid black horizontal line near the bottom spans most of the width as the highway, with a dot and serif italic label 'A' around the middle-right and 'B' near the far right. Two medium-gray dashed rays run from the airplane vertex down to A and to B; the A-ray is noticeably steeper. Two thin black arcs at the vertex open downward from the teal line, the inner/smaller arc labeled '25°' (to B) and the outer arc labeled '49°' (to A), with the 25° label above and left of the 49° label as in the original. In later phases add a thin dashed vertical segment from the plane straight down to the road with a small right-angle square at its foot and the label h, plus horizontal dimension brackets for x (plane's ground point to A) and AB (A to B); render unknown labels in orange and known ones in black. The equation phase shows a light callout box on the right with tan(49°) = h/x and tan(25°) = h/(x + AB). Keep geometry live: angle arcs, ray slopes, and B's position must all recompute from the altitude and AB sliders, with defaults chosen so the displayed angles read ≈49° and ≈25°."
}

